Q1. What is a Docker Image?

Interview Ready Answer

A Docker image is an immutable, read-only template that contains the
application, runtime, libraries, dependencies, and configuration
required to run an application. It is created by building a Dockerfile
using the docker build command and is used to create Docker containers.

Definition

Docker Image
- Read-only template
- Immutable
- Contains application, runtime, dependencies, libraries and configuration
- Used to create Docker containers

Purpose

• Package an application with all its dependencies.
• Ensure consistent execution across different environments.
• Serve as the blueprint for creating containers.

======================================================================

Q2. What are Docker Image Layers?

Interview Ready Answer

A Docker image consists of multiple read-only layers. Each instruction
in a Dockerfile creates a new layer. Docker caches these layers, allowing
unchanged layers to be reused, which improves build speed and reduces
storage usage.

Definition

Image Layer
- Read-only layer.
- Created by each Dockerfile instruction.
- Cached by Docker.

Purpose

• Faster image builds.
• Reduced storage usage.
• Layer sharing between images.

ASCII Diagram

Docker Image
────────────────────────────
Layer 5 : Application Code
────────────────────────────
Layer 4 : Dependencies
────────────────────────────
Layer 3 : JDK
────────────────────────────
Layer 2 : Ubuntu Packages
────────────────────────────
Layer 1 : Base Image
────────────────────────────

