Q3. What is the Container Layer?

Interview Ready Answer

The container layer is the writable layer added on top of the read-only
Docker image when a container is created. Any changes made while the
container is running are stored in this layer without modifying the
original image.

Definition

Container Layer
- Writable layer.
- Exists only for a running container.
- Image remains unchanged.

Purpose

• Store runtime changes.
• Keep the original image immutable.

======================================================================

Q4. What is the difference between a Base Image and a Parent Image?

Interview Ready Answer

A Base Image is the starting image used to build other Docker images and
does not depend on another image.

A Parent Image is an existing image used as the foundation for creating
another image. It may itself be built on top of another image.

Definition

Base Image
- Starting point for image creation.

Parent Image
- Existing image used to build another image.

Purpose

• Enable image reuse.
• Support layered image creation.

======================================================================

Q5. What is the difference between a Docker Registry and a Docker Repository?

Interview Ready Answer

A Docker Registry is a service that stores and distributes Docker images,
such as Docker Hub or a private registry.

A Docker Repository is a collection of related Docker images with
different tags or versions stored within a registry.

Definition

Docker Registry
- Storage service for Docker images.

Docker Repository
- Collection of versions (tags) of the same image.

Purpose

• Registry stores repositories.
• Repository organizes image versions.

