Q6. What is a Dockerfile, and how is it different from a Docker Image?

Interview Ready Answer

A Dockerfile is a text file containing instructions to build a Docker
image, such as copying files, installing dependencies, and configuring
the runtime environment.

A Docker image is the immutable, read-only output produced by executing
those instructions using the docker build command.

Definition

Dockerfile
- Text file.
- Contains build instructions.

Docker Image
- Output of docker build.
- Used to create containers.

Purpose

• Automate image creation.
• Ensure reproducible builds.

ASCII Diagram

Dockerfile
      │
docker build
      │
Docker Image
      │
docker run
      │
Docker Container

